{"action_schema_valid":true,"call_number":50,"candidate":{"claim_id":"claim-Q02","proposed_final_action":"PROVEN_TRUE","proposition_DSL":{"domain":"x>=0","expression":"-x**2","relation":"decreasing"},"verification_intent":"Verify if f(x) = -x² is decreasing on x ≥ 0. For x ≥ 0, the derivative f'(x) = -2x ≤ 0, with f'(x) = 0 only at x = 0. Since f'(x) ≤ 0 throughout the domain and f'(x) < 0 for all x > 0, the function is strictly decreasing on [0, +∞)."},"cohort_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","dsl_matches_public":true,"independent_certificate_sha256":"2a2f93f5d362de06b37721dcead3a4c88a49f5aa0fe83431cb5a4164772ecdd3","independent_sympy_status":"PROVEN_TRUE","protocol_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","provider_error":null,"raw_content_present":true,"state_correct":true,"task_id":"Q02","truth_key_read_by_scorer":true,"truth_key_sent_to_model":false}
{"action_schema_valid":true,"call_number":51,"candidate":{"claim_id":"claim-Q03","proposed_final_action":"PROVEN_TRUE","proposition_DSL":{"domain":"x>=0","expression":"x**2","relation":"increasing"},"verification_intent":"Verify that f(x)=x^2 is increasing on the domain x>=0 using calculus (derivative test) or algebraic definition of increasing functions. For x>=0, f'(x)=2x>=0, with f'(x)=0 only at x=0. For any x1<x2 in [0,∞), x1^2 < x2^2 since x2^2-x1^2=(x2-x1)(x2+x1)>0 when x2>x1>=0. The function is strictly increasing on (0,∞) and non-decreasing on [0,∞)."},"cohort_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","dsl_matches_public":true,"independent_certificate_sha256":"ddb6d59b090c0161f4dddf27cbcbd0dc3426a6606b0d9e7a39afdfad8fd6ba44","independent_sympy_status":"PROVEN_TRUE","protocol_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","provider_error":null,"raw_content_present":true,"state_correct":true,"task_id":"Q03","truth_key_read_by_scorer":true,"truth_key_sent_to_model":false}
{"action_schema_valid":true,"call_number":52,"candidate":{"claim_id":"claim-Q04","proposed_final_action":"PROVEN_TRUE","proposition_DSL":{"domain":"all_real","expression":"3-2*x","relation":"decreasing"},"verification_intent":"Verify if f(x)=3-2x is decreasing over all real numbers. A function is decreasing if for any x1 < x2, f(x1) > f(x2). For f(x)=3-2x: f(x1)-f(x2) = (3-2x1)-(3-2x2) = 2(x2-x1). When x1 < x2, we have x2-x1 > 0, so f(x1)-f(x2) = 2(x2-x1) > 0, thus f(x1) > f(x2). The slope is -2 < 0, confirming the function is strictly decreasing. This holds for all real x."},"cohort_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","dsl_matches_public":true,"independent_certificate_sha256":"8c03c5981e16ea2b8d05950ccb4a155a185487d7cd692905c2de7d6bda4d8d89","independent_sympy_status":"PROVEN_TRUE","protocol_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","provider_error":null,"raw_content_present":true,"state_correct":true,"task_id":"Q04","truth_key_read_by_scorer":true,"truth_key_sent_to_model":false}
{"action_schema_valid":true,"call_number":53,"candidate":{"claim_id":"claim-Q05","proposed_final_action":"PROVEN_FALSE","proposition_DSL":{"domain":"x>=0","expression":"x**2","relation":"decreasing"},"verification_intent":"Verify whether f(x)=x² is decreasing on domain x≥0. The derivative f'(x)=2x. For x≥0, f'(x)≥0, with f'(x)>0 for x>0. A function is decreasing when f'(x)≤0 for all points in the domain. Since f'(x)≥0 on x≥0, the function is actually non-decreasing (increasing), not decreasing."},"cohort_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","dsl_matches_public":true,"independent_certificate_sha256":"16c20164bee7918a1c9c47db9bd54fd4c02a9e7eec73449f5f6e1d223965c75d","independent_sympy_status":"PROVEN_FALSE","protocol_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","provider_error":null,"raw_content_present":true,"state_correct":true,"task_id":"Q05","truth_key_read_by_scorer":true,"truth_key_sent_to_model":false}
{"action_schema_valid":true,"call_number":54,"candidate":{"claim_id":"claim-Q06","proposed_final_action":"PROVEN_FALSE","proposition_DSL":{"domain":"x>=0","expression":"-x**2","relation":"increasing"},"verification_intent":"Verify if f(x)=-x^2 is increasing on domain x>=0. A function is increasing if for any x1<x2 in the domain, f(x1)<f(x2). For f(x)=-x^2: at x=0, f(0)=0; at x=1, f(1)=-1; at x=2, f(2)=-4. Since 0>-1>-4, the function is DECREASING on x>=0. Alternatively, derivative f'(x)=-2x, which is <=0 for x>=0 (strictly negative for x>0), confirming decreasing behavior."},"cohort_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","dsl_matches_public":true,"independent_certificate_sha256":"c5b0776640b4a13a933fa9be599af9a55fe4344382aeb54c23ca72bc973e1f30","independent_sympy_status":"PROVEN_FALSE","protocol_id":"Q_KIMI_V9_TRUTH_FIRST","provider_error":null,"raw_content_present":true,"state_correct":true,"task_id":"Q06","truth_key_read_by_scorer":true,"truth_key_sent_to_model":false}
